    <div class="decoder-modal-content" role="document">
      <button type="button" class="decoder-modal-close" aria-label="Close dialog" data-modal-close>&times;</button>
      <div class="decoder-modal-header">
        <h2 id="howto-guild-title">How to decode a Guild serial number</h2>
        <p class="decoder-modal-subtitle">A quick manual method when you don't want to use a decoder.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="decoder-modal-body">
        <p>Guild guitar serial numbers are generally found on the back of the headstock or inside the soundhole, with decoding methods varying significantly by era. Earlier models (1950s–1970s) typically rely on production date charts, while modern, US‑made Guilds often use a letter prefix for the decade and a subsequent number for the year.</p>

        <h3>Key Eras and Decoding Tips</h3>
        <ul class="decoder-howto-steps">
          <li><strong>1952–1960s:</strong> Serial numbers are sequential; for example, 1953 began around 1000, 1956 around 3000, and 1959 around 4000.</li>
          <li><strong>1965–1969:</strong> Separate, model‑specific serial numbering systems were often used.</li>
          <li><strong>1970–1979:</strong> Guild Guitars provides annual range charts (e.g., 1970: 46696–50978).</li>
          <li><strong>1979–1983:</strong> Returned to model‑specific prefixes.</li>
          <li><strong>Modern US‑Made:</strong> Often, the first letter indicates the decade, followed by numbers for the year.</li>
          <li><strong>Other Potential Formats:</strong> Some serials may feature a letter (factory code), followed by two digits for the year, and a production number.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="decoder-modal-note">For the most accurate identification, it is recommended to check the Guild Guitars website directly for their detailed dating guides.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
